# Avoid optional fields (especially with defaults)

Prefer **required** fields. Use `T | null` when a value can be absent.
Do not paper over missing host decisions with `?` + a library default,
and do not use `?` as a stand-in for “maybe empty”.

## Why

Optional + default often smuggles **host-owned** content into the library:

- English copy (`title = "Library"`, `label = "+ Add item"`)
- Design choices the host should make
- Silent behavior when the caller forgets to pass something

`field?: T` also hides absence: readers cannot tell omit-vs-empty without
checking `in` / `undefined`. A required `field: T | null` makes the
empty case visible at every construction site.

That fights host-agnostic packages (see `no-html-outside-demo.mdc`).

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — library invents product copy; optional hides the requirement
({
  title = "Library",
  addSectionLabel = "+ Add section",
}: {
  title?: string;
  addSectionLabel?: string;
}) => …

// ✅ GOOD — host must pass copy; types enforce it
({
  title,
  addSectionLabel,
}: {
  title: string;
  addSectionLabel: string;
}) => …
```

```tsx
// ✅ Demo / host supplies the strings
<Side title="Library" addSectionLabel="+ Add section" … />
<AddFormItem label="+ Add item" … />
```

## When optional is OK

- Sparse **keys** on a dict (`AdditionalChanges[originId]` missing vs present)
- Narrow escape hatches documented as overrides of a required primary path
  (rare — prefer a separate required prop or a variant type)

Absent **fields on a present object** are `T | null`, not `T?`:

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — omit vs empty is invisible
{ comment?: string; children?: Cols[][] }

// ✅ GOOD — every construction names the empty case
{ comment: string | null; children: Cols[][] | null }
```

## Not a loophole

Do **not** "fix" hardcoded copy by making the field optional with the
same string as default. Either the host owns the value (required) or the
library should not mention that string at all (move it to the demo).

## Prefer split types over `Omit`

When one caller needs a subset of another type's fields, **declare that
subset as its own type** and compose with `&` — do not carve fields out
with `Omit<BigType, "field">`.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — derived hole; the factory's real contract is invisible
renderAddItem: (args: Omit<AddFormItemArgs<…>, "label">) => ReactNode

// ✅ GOOD — named slot args; full args = slot + host label
type AddFormItemSlotArgs<…> = { span; menuItems; random; setAddItem };
type AddFormItemArgs<…> = AddFormItemSlotArgs<…> & { label: string };
renderAddItem: (args: AddFormItemSlotArgs<…>) => ReactNode
```
